# A Postgres connection string. For local development, either run Postgres
# yourself (e.g. `postgresql://postgres:password@localhost:5432/godilly`)
# or use a free hosted instance (Vercel Postgres, Neon, Supabase, ...) — the
# same URL works for both local dev and a deployed instance.
#
# If your provider distinguishes a pooled vs. direct connection string (Neon,
# Supabase, PgBouncer in front of Postgres generally), point this at the
# POOLED one — it's what the app's normal request-time queries use.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/godilly"

# Migrations (`prisma migrate deploy`) need a real session-level Postgres
# lock, which a pooled/PgBouncer connection doesn't reliably support and can
# hang or time out on (Prisma error P1002). Point this at the same database's
# DIRECT (non-pooled) connection string. If your provider has no pooled/direct
# distinction (a plain local Postgres, for instance), just repeat the same
# value as DATABASE_URL.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/godilly"

# Shared secret gating /admin (the content management interface). Set this
# to something long and random before deploying publicly.
ADMIN_SECRET="change-me"

# Optional: real billing via Razorpay Subscriptions. Without these, "Go
# Premium" falls back to a demo toggle (no payment collected). To enable:
# 1. Create a Razorpay account (razorpay.com) — test-mode keys work without
#    full KYC; live payments need KYC approved.
# 2. Dashboard -> Settings -> API Keys -> generate a Key ID + Key Secret.
# 3. Dashboard -> Subscriptions -> Plans -> create a plan (₹99/month, matching
#    the PricingPlan row seeded by prisma/seed.ts) -> copy its plan_id. Plans
#    are immutable once created — a future price change needs a new plan_id.
# 4. Dashboard -> Settings -> Webhooks -> add https://<your-domain>/api/payments/webhook,
#    subscribed to subscription.activated, subscription.charged,
#    subscription.cancelled, subscription.completed, subscription.halted
#    -> copy the webhook secret (different from the API Key Secret above).
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=""
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=""
RAZORPAY_PLAN_ID=""
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Optional: "Sign in with Google" (app/api/auth/google/*). Without these,
# the header just shows no sign-in option and every visitor stays anonymous
# (still fully functional — see README's Persistence & identity section).
# 1. console.cloud.google.com -> create a project (or use an existing one).
# 2. APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen -> configure it (External is
#    fine for a small app; you don't need Google's verification review
#    unless you exceed 100 test users while in "Testing" mode).
# 3. APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID
#    -> Web application. Add BOTH of these as "Authorized redirect URIs"
#    (the app computes the right one from the request automatically):
#      http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#      https://<your-production-domain>/api/auth/google/callback
# 4. Copy the generated Client ID + Client Secret.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Optional: PostHog analytics (return-rate/retention dashboards, beyond
# what lastSeenAt + the SQL in README's Analytics section gives you for
# free). NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix means these are visible in the browser bundle
# — that's expected, PostHog's project key is meant to be public.
# 1. posthog.com -> sign up -> create a project.
# 2. Project Settings -> copy the "Project API Key" and the correct
#    api_host for your region (us.i.posthog.com or eu.i.posthog.com).
# 3. Session replay (heatmaps/recordings) piggybacks on this same key —
#    Project Settings -> Session Replay -> toggle it on. No code change.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST="https://us.i.posthog.com"

# Optional: Microsoft Clarity (components/ClarityProvider.tsx) — free,
# unlimited heatmaps + session recordings + rage-click/dead-click
# detection, in Clarity's own dashboard (separate from PostHog above,
# which is event/funnel analytics rather than heatmaps).
# 1. clarity.microsoft.com -> sign in -> Add new project -> fill in the
#    name and this site's URL.
# 2. The setup page shows your Project ID (also under Settings -> Setup);
#    copy just the id, not the whole script tag.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=""

# Optional: "come back tomorrow" Web Push notifications (app/api/push/*,
# components/NotifyMeButton.tsx, public/sw.js). Without these, the "Remind
# me tomorrow" control just doesn't render — everything else still works.
# Requires Google sign-in to be configured too (see GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above):
# notifications are opt-in per signed-in visitor, not the anonymous cookie.
# 1. Generate a key pair once: `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys --json`.
#    NEXT_PUBLIC_ on the public key means it's in the browser bundle —
#    that's expected, it's not a secret. Keep VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY secret.
# 2. VAPID_SUBJECT identifies you to the push services (Google FCM, Mozilla
#    autopush, etc.) if they ever need to contact you about your usage —
#    "mailto:you@yourdomain.com" or "https://yourdomain.com" both work.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:you@yourdomain.com"

# Shared secret protecting POST /api/push/send-daily (the cron endpoint that
# actually sends that day's reminders) from being called by anyone else.
# Set the same long random string here and as the Authorization header
# Vercel Cron sends — see vercel.json and README's Push notifications
# section for the exact setup.
CRON_SECRET="change-me"
